{"id":17723310818269,"type":"album","name":"Buffalo Springfield Again","artist":"Buffalo Springfield","year":1967,"weight":25,"info":{"cover":"https://dn720706.ca.archive.org/0/items/mbid-369047b4-1e2f-495e-be3a-fca5e8e1059e/mbid-369047b4-1e2f-495e-be3a-fca5e8e1059e-34608795355.jpg","kind":"Album","tracks":10,"country":"US","yt":"HSqJf6JbpPA","links":{"rateyourmusic":"https://rateyourmusic.com/release/album/buffalo-springfield/buffalo-springfield-again"},"desc":"Buffalo Springfield Again is the second studio album by Buffalo Springfield, released in 1967. Expanding beyond their folk-rock roots, the album explores elements of psychedelic rock, baroque pop, and early country rock, showcasing the diverse songwriting talents of Stephen Stills, Neil Young, and Richie Furay. Notable tracks include Mr. Soul, a fuzzed-out garage rocker by Young, the intricate, jazz-tinged Everydays by Stills, and Rock & Roll Woman, which foreshadowed the harmonies and style of Crosby, Stills & Nash. Though internal conflicts plagued the band during recording, Buffalo Springfield Again remains a landmark album, further cementing their influence on the evolution ."},"artist_ref":{"id":4914674385336,"name":"Buffalo Springfield","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Buffalo_Springfield_in_1966.png?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[{"id":185670346256268,"type":"genre","name":"rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":3782,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":18872195504512,"type":"genre","name":"Pop Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":740,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":147423806094006,"type":"genre","name":"Psychedelic Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":690,"yt":null,"src":"musthear,rym"},{"id":194427380844280,"type":"genre","name":"Folk Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":621,"yt":null,"src":"musthear,rym"},{"id":109063683126539,"type":"genre","name":"Hard Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":562,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":90510611927654,"